WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:54.700
[Music]

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.360
[Narrator:] In the short time since the discovery of sulfonamides and then penicillin,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.020
the world has witnessed greater progress

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.329
in the control of infectious diseases in man
and animals

00:01:04.329 --> 00:01:07.280
than has previously occurred in medical history.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.110
The scene is changing with such rapidity

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.690
that what is known today is frequently invalid
tomorrow.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.190
The practitioner must ever keep abreast of
late clinical evidence

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:22.560
in order to exercise sound judgment in employing
antimicrobial agents available to him.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.470
It is the purpose of this film, therefore,
to say, in effect,

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:31.810
this we know, but there is more to learn.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.990
Since the introduction of broad-spectrum antibiotics,

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.030
the dramatic changes in morbidity and mortality
statistics

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.530
have established antimicrobial agents

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:46.540
as one of the major avenues of attack against
infectious diseases.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.320
Tetracycline, demethylchlortetracycline, chlortetracycline,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.110
oxytetracycline, chloramphenicol, streptomycin,

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:00.150
the sulfonamides, or penicillin have been
shown to be effective

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:05.290
in the control of brucellosis, pertussis,
Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever,

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.590
scarlet fever, tuberculosis, tularemia,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.280
typhoid and paratyphoid fever, and typhus.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.920
But valuable as antibiotics are, they cannot
replace accurate diagnosis,

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.880
therapeutic judgment, and skilled surgery.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.540
Infections must be properly treated and anatomic
abnormalities corrected.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:30.920
Even though many infectious diseases may be
controlled by antimicrobial agents,

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.510
many others will either not respond to them,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.170
or of are such nature that it is desirable

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:39.410
to prevent their occurrence by immunization.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.950
Passive immunization against these diseases

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:10.090
may be of value in decreasing the severity
of the symptoms.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.989
Combined immunization against diphtheria, pertussis, and tetanus

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:18.129
should be started at one to two months of
age.

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:23.250
Polio vaccine may be started at the same time
as the other immunizations.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.290
The initial course should consist of three
injections of triple antigen,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.680
given at intervals of not less than one month

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.840
and preferably not more than three months.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.030
It is recommended that a fourth injection
be given

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.750
a year following the third injection.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:42.769
Injections should consist of .5 ml made into
either the deltoid

00:03:42.769 --> 00:03:45.750
or the gluteus maximus muscles.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.590
Injections should not be made more than once

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:53.160
at the same site during the primary course
of immunization.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.040
Those states having the largest number of
diphtheria cases

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.420
are shown in the darkest color.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.060
Reported cases of diphtheria, as might be
expected,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.260
have shown a steady decrease during the last
15 years.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:19.019
Diphtheria infection sources are discharges
and secretions

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:23.350
from the surfaces of the nose, throat, nasal
pharynx, skin,

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.400
and other lesions of infected persons.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.400
A diagnostic test consists of culture rapid
growth of bacteria in four hours

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:37.550
on horse serum-impregnated cotton swab kept
at 37 degrees centigrade

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.900
in Loeffler's or Tellurite media.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.350
Strains isolated should be tested for virulence.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:49.340
For older children and adults, diphtheria
toxoid as a single antigen

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:55.280
is indicated in reduced dosage for primary
immunization and booster injections.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.660
In primary immunization of children in the
preschool age,

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.660
the use of diphtheria toxoid alone is recommended

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.840
only when there is some specific contra-indication

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.470
to the use of the triple antigens.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:12.340
For individuals over 10 years old, diphtheria
toxoid in doses

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.530
exceeding two hundredths to five hundredths
ml

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.270
should not be given without a preliminary
Schick test,

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:24.320
together with a Moloney or [Zella?] toxoid
sensitivity test as a control.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.080
For primary immunization of children less
than 10 years of age,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.750
give three intramuscular injections of five
tenths ml

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:38.430
at intervals of at least one month, but preferably
not more than four months.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:42.610
For individuals more than 10 years of age
who are Schick-positive reactors

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.820
with negative sensitivity tests,

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:51.180
the primary immunization course is the same
as for children under 10.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.960
Individuals showing positive reactions to
both the Schick test

00:05:54.290 --> 00:06:00.370
and a toxoid sensitivity test should not
be given further toxoid.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.970
Negative reactors to both the Schick and toxoid
sensitivity tests

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:09.880
require no further toxoid as the tests themselves
serve as small boosters.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:14.960
But if immunization practices including recall
injections at three-year intervals

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.910
are carried out as previously outlined,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:23.889
repeated use of the Schick test to determine
individual susceptibility seems unwarranted.

00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:28.110
Passive immunity may be obtained for about
10 days with diphtheria antitoxin

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:33.009
in doses of from 1,000 to 2,000 units intramuscularly.

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:35.580
A careful history and adequate sensitivity
testing

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.830
should be done before any animal serum product
is administered.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.720
However, serum sickness may occur in individuals

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.460
who had no sensitivity prior to serum administration.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.810
Whooping cough sources of infection are from discharges

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:58.009
from pharyngeal and bronchial mucus membranes of infected persons.

00:06:58.009 --> 00:07:01.280
Pertussis vaccine as a single antigen is indicated

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.069
for attempted rapid primary protection of
infants

00:07:04.069 --> 00:07:07.460
in the presence of epidemics, for primary
immunization

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:11.340
of children who have been inoculated against
diphtheria and tetanus,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.600
and for recall injections following exposure.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:19.600
Pertussis vaccine, saline suspended, is useful
in attempted rapid protection

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.180
of exposed individuals during epidemics.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:27.500
A total of 12 NIH units divided into three
equal doses

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.979
should be injected subcutaneously at intervals
of one week.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:37.100
Pertussis vaccine, alum-precipitated or aluminum
phosphate- or hydroxide-absorbed

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.039
are preferred for infants less than six months

00:07:40.039 --> 00:07:44.270
when there is no need for rapid protection,
and when he has already received

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.889
diphtheria and tetanus inoculations,

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:51.830
or a contra-indication to the triple antigen
exists.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:55.180
Twelve NIH units should be injected intramuscularly

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:59.289
in three equal doses at intervals of four
to six weeks.

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:05.229
Recall injections of four NIH units should
be given a year later, then in two years,

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:10.000
and thereafter every three years if the danger
of whooping cough infection exists.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.490
Against tetanus, active immunization is indicated in all children,

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:21.419
adults subject to abrasions, and in adults
who are exposed to frequent accidents

00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:25.099
where tetanus spores may contaminate the wound.

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:28.349
Primary immunization is obtained by two intramuscular
injections

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:35.190
of .5 ml each of alum-precipitated or aluminum
hydroxide-adsorbed tetanus toxoid

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.969
at an interval of between one and four months.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:44.350
Recall injections are advised in one year
and every five to 10 years thereafter,

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.249
or after potential exposure.

00:08:46.249 --> 00:08:50.879
The effectiveness of emergency boosters is
dependent on the time relationship

00:08:50.879 --> 00:08:54.699
between the injection and the potential development
of infection

00:08:54.699 --> 00:08:57.579
so that toxoid alone may not be adequate.

00:08:57.579 --> 00:09:01.300
When the individual already has had previous
toxoid,

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.480
added protection is afforded if, in addition
to the booster,

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:07.670
he is given hyperimmune gamma globulin

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:11.749
in the muscles of a different extremity using
the usual precautions.

00:09:11.749 --> 00:09:14.959
Where the possibility of gas gangrene coexists,

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:18.399
a gas gangrene antitoxin is also available.

00:09:20.589 --> 00:09:23.939
Although smallpox is endemic in various parts of the world,

00:09:23.939 --> 00:09:26.639
it has virtually been eliminated from the
United States.

00:09:30.329 --> 00:09:33.709
Individuals suspected of smallpox should be isolated strictly,

00:09:33.709 --> 00:09:37.119
pending definite diagnosis.

00:09:37.119 --> 00:09:40.059
Diagnosis on clinical evidence is generally
possible

00:09:40.059 --> 00:09:42.399
and preventive measures should be instituted

00:09:42.399 --> 00:09:46.860
without waiting for results of laboratory
tests.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.779
Primary vaccination is recommended in the
first year of life

00:09:49.779 --> 00:09:53.910
unless the child is not thriving or is suffering
from allergic eczema

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:59.199
or dermatologic disorders, with revaccination
every three years thereafter.

00:09:59.199 --> 00:10:04.239
The multiple pressure method should be used
over the skin of the left deltoid muscle

00:10:04.239 --> 00:10:08.249
or the posterior aspect of the arm over the
triceps muscle.

00:10:08.249 --> 00:10:12.239
These are the stages of successful primary
vaccination:

00:10:12.239 --> 00:10:14.999
maculation, second day;

00:10:14.999 --> 00:10:18.379
papulation, fourth day;

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:22.739
vesiculation, sixth day;

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:28.900
pustulation, tenth day;

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:32.939
scabbing or crusting, thirteenth day;

00:10:32.939 --> 00:10:38.209
scarring, twentieth day.

00:10:38.209 --> 00:10:40.769
This is an early or immediate reaction.

00:10:44.619 --> 00:10:48.549
Tuberculin-negative individuals who are potentially exposed to tuberculosis

00:10:48.549 --> 00:10:51.360
can be immunized with the BCG vaccine.

00:10:58.989 --> 00:11:02.709
Tuberculin sensitivity is determined by means of the intracutaneous injection

00:11:02.709 --> 00:11:08.769
of OT or PPD or by the use of the tuberculin
tine test shown here.

00:11:08.769 --> 00:11:12.290
As soon as possible after completing the tuberculin
test,

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:17.769
.1 ml of material equal to .1 mg BCG vaccine

00:11:17.769 --> 00:11:20.110
should be injected intracutaneously

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:23.899
as superficially as possible over the deltoid
muscle.

00:11:23.899 --> 00:11:28.709
BCG vaccine should not be given to infants
who are markedly underweight at birth

00:11:28.709 --> 00:11:31.529
or who are premature, nor should it be given

00:11:31.529 --> 00:11:34.480
when the person is suffering from infectious
diseases.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:38.629
Although the vaccine may be given at the same
time with other antigens,

00:11:38.629 --> 00:11:42.819
it should never be administered simultaneously
with smallpox vaccination.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:49.360
Typhoid fever sources of infection are the
feces and urine of patients and carriers.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.680
Immunization is indicated for residents in
rural areas

00:11:54.689 --> 00:12:00.779
of the United States and Canada where the
disease is prevalent.

00:12:00.779 --> 00:12:05.519
For adults, a total dose of 1 ml standard
typhoid vaccine should be given

00:12:05.519 --> 00:12:12.339
in two equal doses one to four weeks apart,
by the subcutaneous route.

00:12:12.339 --> 00:12:17.129
Infants less than one year of age should receive
correspondingly small doses.

00:12:17.129 --> 00:12:22.470
Sharp febrile reactions are not uncommon.

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:25.480
Recall injections of .1 ml intracutaneously

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.619
every one to two years depending on probability
of exposure.

00:12:33.759 --> 00:12:39.259
Active immunization against scarlet fever,
streptococcal pharyngitis, and arrecipolis

00:12:39.259 --> 00:12:41.730
is no longer attempted since the advent of

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.609
effective chemotherapy of streptococcal diseases.

00:12:47.389 --> 00:12:50.920
Adequate treatment of streptococcal infections
with one of the penicillins

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:55.189
has become most important, since the development
of acute glomerulonephritis

00:12:55.189 --> 00:13:01.069
and rheumatic fever may be prevented in this
way.

00:13:01.069 --> 00:13:03.839
Passive immunization has been practiced in
the past,

00:13:03.839 --> 00:13:08.019
but the advent of antibiotics makes the procedure
unnecessary.

00:13:08.019 --> 00:13:12.819
In certain circumstances, it may be desirable
to culture exposed individuals

00:13:12.819 --> 00:13:15.149
and treat those with positive cultures,

00:13:15.149 --> 00:13:19.779
especially if the index case developed nephritis.

00:13:19.779 --> 00:13:21.739
Antimicrobial therapy should be seriously
considered

00:13:21.739 --> 00:13:24.449
for administration to hospitalized patients

00:13:24.449 --> 00:13:27.410
as well as to close contacts of cases.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:30.359
The importance of careful investigation and
adequate treatment

00:13:30.359 --> 00:13:33.790
of any contact having a history of rheumatic
fever is well-known.

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:40.100
Prophylactic use of penicillin and other antimicrobial
agents is often necessary.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:46.919
Satisfactory immunity against poliomyelitis
has been conferred

00:13:46.919 --> 00:13:52.009
in several large mass vaccination programs
by the administration of two feedings

00:13:52.239 --> 00:13:56.790
of trivalent Sabin vaccine at intervals of
one to two months

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:02.730
or by the feeding of monovalent vaccine at
intervals of one month.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:06.759
The precise need for boosters has not yet
been clearly shown,

00:14:06.759 --> 00:14:10.829
although recent evidence indicates that it
may be prudent to administer

00:14:10.829 --> 00:14:15.540
booster feedings to children three years after
the primary series.

00:14:19.269 --> 00:14:22.459
In the case of measles, passive immunization may be attempted

00:14:22.459 --> 00:14:27.079
when conditions render it advisable, such
as in infants under 12 months,

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:30.759
children ill with or closely exposed to other
diseases

00:14:30.759 --> 00:14:32.769
such as tuberculosis and rheumatic fever.

00:14:34.069 --> 00:14:39.139
Killed vaccine or gamma globulin is advised for such patients.

00:14:39.139 --> 00:14:47.149
Passive immunization may be used in institutions to avoid epidemics.

00:14:47.149 --> 00:14:51.369
Clinical experience indicates that .02 ml
of human gamma globulin

00:14:51.369 --> 00:14:55.149
per pound of body weight is satisfactory for modification,

00:14:55.149 --> 00:15:00.269
while .1 ml per pound of body weight is considered
sufficient for prevention.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:08.879
Passive immunization against, or attenuation
of, infectious hepatitis

00:15:08.879 --> 00:15:11.799
may be attempted in cases where infection
is likely

00:15:11.799 --> 00:15:15.699
or exposure is known to have occurred.

00:15:15.699 --> 00:15:20.230
Human gamma globulin injected subcutaneously
or intramuscularly

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:25.970
is thought to give protection for six to eight
weeks.

00:15:26.879 --> 00:15:29.189
Effective dosage range has been found to lie
between

00:15:29.189 --> 00:15:34.749
.01 ml and .06 ml per pound of body weight.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.879
Temporary immunity can be obtained by injection of gamma globulin

00:15:41.879 --> 00:15:49.209
prepared from donors who have been hyperimmunized with mumps vaccine.

00:15:49.209 --> 00:15:51.149
Active immunization can be accomplished

00:15:51.149 --> 00:15:55.350
by means of subcutaneous injections of inactivated vaccines.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.799
The recent attenuation of the mumps virus

00:15:57.799 --> 00:16:01.699
may make routine immunization of infants and
children possible.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:12.720
Brucellosis infections in man should be prevented

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:15.829
through pasteurization of all dairy products,

00:16:15.829 --> 00:16:18.819
separation of hogs from milk-producing cattle,

00:16:18.819 --> 00:16:24.049
and veterinary measures to eliminate brucellosis
from cattle and hogs.

00:16:35.819 --> 00:16:38.369
Active immunization against Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever

00:16:38.369 --> 00:16:42.959
is advised only for individuals working in
highly endemic areas.

00:16:47.439 --> 00:16:51.009
Protection from vaccination is not long-lasting.

00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:57.389
Stimulating doses must be given annually to
maintain a high level of protection.

00:16:57.389 --> 00:17:03.600
Three subcutaneous injections of .5 ml each
of inactivated Cox yolk-sac vaccine

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.410
should be given at five- to seven-day intervals.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:10.519
The case you have seen here is typical of
the disease.

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.189
Active immunization against tularemia is advised

00:17:18.189 --> 00:17:20.079
only for laboratory workers

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:24.439
and those handling wild rabbits and other
susceptible animals.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:27.520
Individuals should be skin-tested prior to
vaccination

00:17:27.529 --> 00:17:31.062
[Line graph of reported cases of tularemia,
1950-1965]

00:17:27.529 --> 00:17:33.470
using one to 1,000 dilution of vaccine and
saline.

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:35.669
Positive reactors should not be immunized

00:17:35.669 --> 00:17:38.909
as they may develop severe reactions.

00:17:38.909 --> 00:17:42.950
Negative reactors should be given three doses
of vaccine subcutaneously

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:51.000
at two-day intervals in the following amounts:
.1 ml, .5 ml and .5 ml.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:58.200
Rabies in man has decreased in recent years

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:03.230
to the point that only six cases have been
reported since 1961.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:07.360
After exposure to rabies, patients with bites
about the trunk or extremities

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:11.440
with no extensive tissue damage should be
given rabies vaccine

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:17.419
in .5 ml doses subcutaneously daily for 14
days.

00:18:17.419 --> 00:18:20.970
In cases of extensive tissue damage, multiple
bites,

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:24.850
injuries about the head, or where treatment
has been delayed,

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:30.730
2.5 ml doses should be given daily for the
next seven days.

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:36.049
Anti-rabies serum is a useful adjunct to therapy
with rabies vaccine

00:18:36.049 --> 00:18:37.580
for the prevention of rabies,

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:42.700
but should not be used as a substitute for
the vaccine.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:45.850
It is of vital importance that the dog be
caught.

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:48.429
If it dies after showing signs of rabies,

00:18:48.429 --> 00:18:53.440
its brain should be examined for the presence
of Negri bodies or fluorescent antibodies.

00:18:57.639 --> 00:19:02.299
Cholera, an important disease in the United
States until 1893,

00:19:02.299 --> 00:19:06.559
is now confined to southeastern Asia, China, and the near east.

00:19:06.559 --> 00:19:10.840
A killed vaccine is available for people entering endemic zones,

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:16.040
but the immunity conferred thereby is of relatively short duration.

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:20.400
Immunization is obtained in adults with a
.5 ml injection of

00:19:20.409 --> 00:19:23.309
inactivated cholera vaccine subcutaneously

00:19:23.309 --> 00:19:27.440
followed a week later by an injection of 1
ml.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:35.049
For children under 11 the following doses
are recommended.

00:19:35.049 --> 00:19:42.789
Booster injections of from .1 ml to 1 ml according
to age may be required every six months.

00:19:47.139 --> 00:19:50.190
Plague occurs in wild rodents and/or in man

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.700
in many parts of the world.

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:58.850
In the United States, the endemicity in wild
rodents is slowly moving eastward.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:00.159
Human cases are rare,

00:20:00.159 --> 00:20:04.880
about 500 cases having been reported since
1900.

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:12.480
This is a bubo showing extensive necrosis
and bubo pasteurella pestis in large numbers.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:17.870
Plague immunization may be obtained in adults
with a .5 ml injection of plague vaccine

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:23.389
subcutaneously, followed by 1 ml given 7 to 28 days later.

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.850
For children under 11, three subcutaneous injections

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:32.580
at 7- to 10-day intervals should be given as shown.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:37.120
Re-immunization may be required not more often than every four to six months

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:40.060
where plague is a hazard.

00:20:43.029 --> 00:20:46.789
Basic typhus immunization may be obtained in adults

00:20:46.789 --> 00:20:50.379
by a 1 ml subcutaneous injection of typhus vaccine

00:20:50.379 --> 00:20:56.139
followed by a second injection of 1 ml given in one to three weeks

00:20:56.139 --> 00:20:58.529
and a third in the same amount a year later.

00:21:00.559 --> 00:21:06.610
Children under 11 should be given equal doses
of vaccine one to three weeks apart.

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:10.159
The dose for a six-month old child to a two-year
old should be

00:21:10.159 --> 00:21:20.860
.1 ml; three to six years .25 ml; seven to
ten years .5 ml.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:25.169
Boosters are from .12 ml to 1 ml according
to age

00:21:25.169 --> 00:21:28.070
should be given annually to retain immunity.

00:21:32.409 --> 00:21:36.759
This is the reported distribution of human
cases of yellow fever.

00:21:36.759 --> 00:21:38.889
Cases in jungle animals are important

00:21:38.889 --> 00:21:41.309
because they may under certain circumstances

00:21:41.309 --> 00:21:45.809
feed urban epidemics among man.

00:21:45.809 --> 00:21:48.280
Immunization for all age groups is obtained

00:21:48.289 --> 00:21:52.960
by a single subcutaneous injection of .5 ml
of a

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:57.240
one to 10 dilution of concentrated yellow
fever vaccine.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:59.169
Containing an attenuated strain of virus,

00:21:59.169 --> 00:22:05.469
a similar dose as a booster may be required
every six years.

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:09.800
These are intranuclear inclusion bodies in
liver cells.

00:22:11.529 --> 00:22:14.059
The administration of an injection for immunization

00:22:14.059 --> 00:22:17.179
against an infectious disease is a simple
procedure,

00:22:17.179 --> 00:22:19.889
but to avoid infecting the patient,

00:22:19.889 --> 00:22:24.580
it is essential that asepsis be maintained
at all times.

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:28.590
Store vaccines and serum products under refrigeration.

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:32.919
Reconstitute live attenuated vaccines just
prior to use.

00:22:32.919 --> 00:22:37.860
Rubber stoppers of the antigen containers
must be disinfected.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:40.500
The physician should be familiar with recent
medical literature

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:46.019
and with package literature before administering
any biological product,

00:22:46.019 --> 00:22:47.929
and every precaution must be taken

00:22:47.929 --> 00:22:53.860
for the prevention and arrest of untoward
reactions.

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:57.649
Serum therapy should be employed only when
definitely indicated

00:22:57.649 --> 00:23:00.299
and only when the physician is equipped adequately

00:23:00.299 --> 00:23:04.679
to combat such reactions as may occur.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:28.400
[Music]

